<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#internal-passport">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Internal passport</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as domestic passport</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An internal or domestic passport is an identity document in passport form, used variously for identification, civil registration, recording ethnicity or status, and controlling internal migration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> required one to leave one's canton from 1795, and former convicts carried a yellow passport that made them outcasts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>'s system was abandoned after 1917, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> reintroduced the passport regime in 1932, binding people to their place of residence through the propiska. Others served discrimination: passes for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in the slave states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s pass system for First Nations people from 1885, and apartheid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s pass books.</p></div>
